@mkbrewer, @ayshih - OK, yes, that really helps!
@mkbrewer - I opened #10271 for the inclusion of the location of the observer on Earth. As you note, it is a very small effect, but I agree it is missing and we should document that at the very least.
I do share @ayshih's worry about how to actually do the travel-time correction in practice. But I think the simpler part of the request is to implement the "astrometric" frame that sits in between BCRS and GCRS in Fig. 1 of SOFA's astrometric tools; with that, one could at least more easily do the calculations oneself. Is that correct? And would its standard name indeed be "Astrometric"?
p.s. The second frame also seems eminently do-able; it seems our use of the internal _apparent_position_in_true_coordinates() routine is in fact simply to facilitate comparison with skyfield...  I think the main work may be in documenting when the various frames of reference are useful (I find the SOFA guide hard-going). And perhaps refer to skyfield for purposes where that is the better tool (we already rely on jplephem for the JPL ephemerides).

I agree with the concern @ayshih raised, although I also find myself messing up input parameters and would like to have a more helpful docstring. Don't see myself using SkyCoord though, base frames serve me well in poliastro and I don't want a higher level interface.
Perhaps this is only tangentially related, but I wonder if we could move towards offering classmethods with a simpler API. For example:
class ICRS:
    @classmethod
    def from_representation(cls, data, **kwargs): ...
    @classmethod
    def from_spherical(cls, ra, dec, **kwargs): ...
I did not evaluate all the implications of this (hopefully we wouldn't have to do it in every frame subclass). Even if they are kept private at first, it would simplify the __init__ code a lot (which is a pain point at least for me, see my so far unsuccessful #10788)

