OK, thanks both!  I think the first step then simply is to require NamedUnit for .decompose() (and thus for the bases for CompositeUnit). @adrn, I haven't heard from you yet, but related to #1438, it would mean that you would no longer be able to do,
const.G.decompose([u.kg, u.km, u.Unit("100 s")])

However, you would be able to do it if you gave the unit a name, i.e., the following would (continue to) work,
const.G.decompose([u.kg, u.km, u.Unit("hundredsecond", 100*u.s)])

More generally, so would anything defined with def_unit, which probably is often what is needed in practice.

The example signature I wrote before wasn't quite right. First of all bases should be a collection of NamedUnit instances, not UnitBase instances. And if UnitBase is a generic type then that should be reflected in the return annotation of decompose() too. A better outline would be
class UnitBase[PT: PhysicalType]:

    ...

    def decompose(
        self,
        bases: Collection[
            NamedUnit[
                Length, Mass, Time, ElectricCurrent, Temperature, AmountOfSubstance, LuminousIntensity
            ]
        ] = (),
    ) -> UnitBase[PT]:
        ...
